{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB01377 - MONDO_1567",
  "verbalization": "Magnesium Oxide is associated with Nephrocalcinosis through a chain of indications that involve hypercalcemia and calcium carbonate or calcium acetate. The evidence suggests that Magnesium Oxide may contribute to Nephrocalcinosis by inducing hypercalcemia, which in turn promotes the formation of calcium deposits in the kidneys. This mechanism is supported by the presence of multiple pathways linking Magnesium Oxide to hypercalcemia and then to Nephrocalcinosis. The overlapping indications—such as the involvement of calcium carbonate or calcium acetate—highlight a potential role for these compounds in the pathogenesis of Nephrocalcinosis. The integrated evidence implies that Magnesium Oxide may act as a mediator in the development of Nephrocalcinosis by influencing calcium metabolism, though the exact mechanism remains to be fully elucidated.",
